(vector (map (:db/id -1)
  (:adr/id "adr-2809060410-fork-evidence-warrant-seam")
  (:adr/title "Fork evidence as signed warrants reusing the existing seam (gap note + draft)")
  (:adr/status "proposed")
  (:adr/date "2026-09-06")
  (:adr/related (vector "ADR-2809052250-effect-consistency-classification-draft" "ADR-kotoba-rad-git-sovereign-repo"))
  (:adr/context
  "Task: peer-detected double-history must produce signed evidence (warrant), not global canonicalization. Surveyed this tick, all reads from origin/main: kotoba-lang 232182d, nekko 67ada96. Findings: (1) kotoba-lang/src/kotoba/lang/consensus_order.cljc admit-commit! detects fork/out-of-order (throw :consensus/order-invalid at line 88) but discards the evidence in an exception - no signed artifact is produced. (2) kotoba-lang/src/kotoba/lang/ocapn_handoff.cljc already has the signing mechanism: Ed25519 signed Syrup envelopes (sig-val record, sign-envelope!, envelope-parts verification) - interoperable OCapN signatures. (3) incidence_ocapn.cljc durable-receipt (line 127) already draws the evidence/authority boundary: 'inert evidence, not authority'. (4) A warrant vocabulary already EXISTS by design: docs/adr/ADR-kotoba-rad-git-sovereign-repo.md defines :warrant.issue (publish signed evidence of invalid event) with violation->warrant rules (InvalidSignature, SeqBreak, PrevMismatch, CacaoInvalid, ProllyInconsistent). But the repo was renamed kotoba-rad -> nekko (2026-07-16, per west manifest) and grep of nekko origin/main 67ada96 finds NO warrant implementation in src/ - only ref_event.cljc:113 :invalid-event. So: detection point, signing mechanism, and vocabulary all exist; they have never been connected. No new subsystem is needed.")
  (:adr/decision
  "1. Reuse the warrant vocabulary from ADR-kotoba-rad-git-sovereign-repo as the fork-evidence format; do not invent a parallel fork-evidence format. New violation kinds added only where the warrant table lacks coverage: OrderInvalid (consensus_order fork detection) and EntryOrderMismatch. 2. The seam: consensus_order.admit-commit! on order-invalid returns/emits a warrant envelope instead of only throwing - the envelope carries {warrant/kind, warrant/dataspace, warrant/height, warrant/parent-id, warrant/commit-id, warrant/peer} and is signed with the ocapn_handoff Ed25519 signed Syrup envelope mechanism. 3. Warrants are inert evidence per the existing incidence_ocapn boundary: possessing or relaying a warrant never grants authority; adjudication (whether the accused peer is penalized) is out of scope of this ADR. 4. No canonicalization: a warrant does not resolve the fork; it only makes the detection durable and attributable, consistent with 'fork detection produces signed evidence rather than global canonicalization'. 5. Landing path (each gated on its own verification): (a) nekko-side warrant envelope + sign/verify round-trip test; (b) consensus_order wiring so order-invalid emits the signed warrant; (c) compiler-manifest :consistency classes (ADR-2809052250) reference warrants as what a peer signs at :validated-claim disputes. This ADR is a draft: nothing lands until (a) passes a test.")
  (:adr/consequences
  "The gap is now named with an implementation path rather than remaining an ambient design intention. Renamed-repo drift is documented: the warrant design survives in a docs ADR of the old kotoba-rad name while nekko src/ has no implementation - future readers should not assume warrants are implemented. Risk recorded: the warrant table lives in a .md doc in a child repo whose policy file is elsewhere; if it is migrated, the violation-kind names here must follow. No code changed under this ADR.")))
